// The receiver rule holds for a type parameter too: `T.m(…)` reaches only what the bound's trait
// declares `static`. `Named` does not, so a `T` may have nothing to bind `self` to and there is no
// value at the call to become one — E0047.
//
// The reason is the **declaration**, not a survey of implementors. It used to be the survey: the
// checker enumerated every type in the linked program that implements the bound and asked each
// one's body whether it needed a receiver. Two things were wrong with that. It is action at a
// distance — an `impl` in a module this file never names decides whether this body compiles — and
// it is not a question that can be cached against any one declaration. And it is not even
// conservative enough to be sound as written: a self-less implementation (or a self-less *default*)
// proves nothing about the override somebody adds later.
//
// So the fact moved into the contract, where arity, parameter types, return type and `async`-ness
// already live: `static fn label(): string` in the trait, checked once per impl (E0015), trusted at
// every use. Adding the modifier here is the fix the help line names.
//
// Still reported at the DEFINITION site rather than at whichever call site instantiates it: the
// generic body is what promises something the bound does not guarantee.
// expect: error E0047 at 26:42
// expect: exit 1
trait Named { fn label(): string }
struct Thing {
    v: int
    impl Named { fn label(): string { return "thing ${self.v}" } }
}
fn describe<T: Named>(): string { return T.label() }
echo describe::<Thing>()
